@nithishreddy5771 Thanks — good catch, and the repro description was exactly what was needed. Fixed on this branch: the run event handler (`createAgentRunEventHandler`, one instance per turn) now dedupes the `context_bounded` warning, so a byte-bounded turn that retries after a provider error delivers the notice once per turn instead of once per attempt.

Added a regression test mirroring your repro in `agent-runner-execution-compaction.test.ts` ("delivers the context_bounded warning once per turn across retries"): two `context_bounded` events in one turn deliver the notice exactly once. It fails without the fix (notice delivered twice) and passes with it; the full file is 13/13 green. The test-lines allocation is bumped 123 → 140 to cover the new test, and both ratchets pass locally.

If your regression test differs from mine, happy to align — otherwise this should cover the edge case.

Fix design for the participant-recording half (problem 2), from reading the current dispatch code — posting before coding because the admission-skip path has no existing test coverage and the harness work deserves an owner decision:

**Where.** `finishReplyOperationBusyDispatch` in `src/auto-reply/reply/dispatch-from-config.prepare-context.ts` is the exact discard point for the `reply-operation-active` skip (it runs after target resolution: `sessionStoreEntry` (with `agentId`/`sessionKey`/`storePath`) and `ctx` — which already carries the participant prepared by `chat-send-user-turn.ts` via `prepareSessionParticipantInput` — are both in scope, and the recording owner's contract (`recordAcceptedSessionParticipantInput`: "call only after admission and final target selection; never creates a session") is satisfied there. Recording in `chat.send`'s persist stage instead would be wrong: the final session target is not yet resolved at that point (bindings/resets can retarget), which is exactly why the recording is deferred to `initSessionState` today.

**What.**

```ts
if (!recorder || recorder.hasPersisted()) {
  recordAcceptedSessionParticipantInput(ctx, {
    agentId: sessionStoreEntry.agentId ?? sessionAgentId,
    sessionKey: sessionStoreEntry.sessionKey ?? sessionKey,
    storePath: sessionStoreEntry.storePath,
  });
}
```

The `hasPersisted` guard matters: the webchat user-turn recorder can hold the message as pending input at dispatch time, and a released pending input may be retried (and recorded then); recording it on the skip would create participants for messages that never land. For channel-inbound dispatches there is no recorder and the message is already persisted before dispatch, so they record unconditionally.

**Why not record for the restart-recovery path here.** The recovery turn (`internal_system` provenance) has its own context and only knows `sourceRunId` — it would have to resolve the source run's sender from the transcript before it can record, which is a separate change (and interacts with the recovery-admission question in your second bullet, which needs maintainer confirmation first).

**Test gap.** `finishReplyOperationBusyDispatch` currently has no direct test coverage (no test drives the busy admission refusal), so this needs a new dispatch-from-config harness case: hold a running reply operation for the session (the `createReplyOperation(...).setPhase("running")` pattern from `dispatch-from-config.owner-metadata.test.ts`), dispatch with a prepared profile participant, and assert the participant row lands (via `waitForSessionParticipantRecording` + store readback) after the skipped result.

Fixed the P2 finding on the exact head (b27689cd9a9 → 7be8182ab8d): empty positional slots and `kind: "unknown"` facts are no longer counted as media, matching the persisted media fact contract (`path`/`url`/`contentType`/known `kind` required); record-shaped empty slots keep the `invalid_content` rejection with two new regression cases, and a reference-backed entry without a type labels as `unknown type`.

On the end-to-end proof request: this environment has no live Codex harness credentials (OpenAI Codex app-server login) or Telegram pairing, so I cannot produce the Gateway-run recovery output. The red/green unit evidence covers the projector boundary that owns the rejection; noting the gap explicitly rather than simulating it.

